{"name":"Nymph’s Delight","hex":"#7b6c8e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nymph","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any female nature spirit associated with water, forests, grottos, wind, etc.","A young girl, especially one who is attractive, beautiful or graceful."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fish using a nymph larva as bait."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nymph"},{"term":"delight","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Joy; pleasure.","Something that gives great joy or pleasure."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To give delight to; to affect with great pleasure; to please highly.","To have or take great pleasure."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/delight"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nymph","blocks":[{"text":"A nymph is a minor female nature deity in ancient Greek folklore. Distinct from other Greek goddesses, nymphs are generally regarded as personifications of nature; they are typically tied to a specific place, landform, or tree, and are usually depicted as maidens. Because of their association with springs, they were often seen as having healing properties; other divine powers of the nymphs included divination and shapeshifting. In spite of their divine nature, they were not immortal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Three_nymphs%2C_marble_relief%2C_c._320%E2%80%93300_BC.jpg/330px-Three_nymphs%2C_marble_relief%2C_c._320%E2%80%93300_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nymph","wikidata":"Q373916"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/John_William_Waterhouse_-_Hylas_and_the_Nymphs.jpg/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_Hylas_and_the_Nymphs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/John_William_Waterhouse_-_Hylas_and_the_Nymphs.jpg/500p